r als Instanz dieser Klasse eingegeben werden kann. Zum Beispiel kann angegeben werden, dass die Beobachtung eine Vorhersage von KI ist. Wir bieten auch einen Verweis auf die Klasse MachineLearningModel von ML-DCAT, so dass ein zus\u00E4tzlicher Sensor als Instanz dieser Klasse eingegeben werden kann. Zum Beispiel kann angegeben werden, dass die Beobachtung eine Vorhersage von KI ist."@de-t-en-t0-mtec ,
		"The application profile Energy management system shows how, with terms from existing application profiles and vocabularies, energy monitoring and control systems can be described.A number of terms are reused from ISO19156-2023 OMS,SSN/SOSA-2025,SAREF,IMKLenMLD-DCAT.An energy management system (EMS) is an automated system that optimizes energy consumption in a building, home or industrial complex. This is done by real-time monitoring and measurement, analysis of trends and peak consumption and inefficiencies, optimization and saving by smart algorithms and AI, automatic control of devices and systems (heating, lighting).Earlier than describing the EMS itself, this application profile allows to document the actions carried out by it. These actions are Observations and Controls, e.g. the observation of the energy consumption of the appliance and its acquisition or disposal in function.These actions are described in a structured manner as provided for in ISO19156-2023 OMS, SSN/SOSA-2025 from which they were derived. This means that, in addition to the result vd Observation/Management, the Observation/ManageableKnowledge must be specified (e.g. consumption, on- or off-mode), as well as the Object that has that feature (e.g. Connection or Device), the procedure followed during observation or control (e.g. on the basis of which energy consumption threshold a Device is set) and finally the Control System or the Sensor used in it.The ability to indicate the Input used in the Observation/Management procedure allows a sequence of Observation and/or Control actions to be described, where the result of an Observation/Management constitutes the input for a subsequent Observation/Management procedure. We also provide Observation collection from SSN/SOA so that Observations can be grouped together as Input. Input can also be a threshold or rule (the modelling of rules is outside scope, see RIF,N3,RuleML,IDPedm).This approach allows a lot of EMS use-cases to be described. For example, the shutdown of a production line (Control) based on the day-ahead electricity price (Observation) and a threshold (Input vd Control) and the calculation of the savings (Observation) based on the average consumption of the production line (Observation). Or classifying a building (Observation) according to its average heat loss (Observation), based on the measured heat loss of several representative spaces in the building (multiple Observations). See the data examples in github (explained in the modelling report) that illustrate this principle.The observedObject/controlledObject may be an instance of Object or an instance of any class by substitution of Object. However, if applicable, a subclass thereof may be used, ttz an instance of Connection or Device (or Subclass Meter of Device). Connection is borrowed from IMKL, Appliance and Meter are defined by SAREF.Note that Appliance, but also Control System and Sensor Systems are. The latter are non-disjoint with Toestel, a Sensor bvb so additionally when Toestel are typed and combine characteristics of both classes. Appliances as well as Connections are also potentially Platforms, ttz that they can host other Systems, e.g. an Meter on a Connection, or a Controller or Sensor on a Device.We also provide a reference to the class MachineLearningModel from ML-DCAT, so that a Sensor can be additionally typed as an instance of this class. For example, it can be indicated that the Observation is a prediction made by AI. Terms are reused from ISO19156-2023 OMS,SSN/SOSA-2025,SAREF,IMKLenMLD-DCAT.An energy management system (EMS) is an automated system that optimizes energy consumption in a building, home or industrial complex. This is done by real-time monitoring and measurement, analysis of trends and peak consumption and inefficiencies, optimization and saving by smart algorithms and AI, automatic control of devices and systems (heating, lighting).Earlier than describing the EMS itself, this application profile allows to document the actions carried out by it. These actions are Observations and Controls, e.g. the observation of the energy consumption of the appliance and its acquisition or disposal in function.These actions are described in a structured manner as provided for in ISO19156-2023 OMS, SSN/SOSA-2025 from which they were derived. This means that, in addition to the result vd Observation/Management, the Observation/ManageableKnowledge must be specified (e.g. consumption, on- or off-mode), as well as the Object that has that feature (e.g. Connection or Device), the procedure followed during observation or control (e.g. on the basis of which energy consumption threshold a Device is set) and finally the Control System or the Sensor used in it.The ability to indicate the Input used in the Observation/Management procedure allows a sequence of Observation and/or Control actions to be described, where the result of an Observation/Management constitutes the input for a subsequent Observation/Management procedure. We also provide Observation collection from SSN/SOA so that Observations can be grouped together as Input. Input can also be a threshold or rule (the modelling of rules is outside scope, see RIF,N3,RuleML,IDPedm).This approach allows a lot of EMS use-cases to be described. For example, the shutdown of a production line (Control) based on the day-ahead electricity price (Observation) and a threshold (Input vd Control) and the calculation of the savings (Observation) based on the average consumption of the production line (Observation). Or classifying a building (Observation) according to its average heat loss (Observation), based on the measured heat loss of several representative spaces in the building (multiple Observations). See the data examples in github (explained in the modelling report) that illustrate this principle.The observedObject/controlledObject may be an instance of Object or an instance of any class by substitution of Object. However, if applicable, a subclass thereof may be used, ttz an instance of Connection or Device (or Subclass Meter of Device). Connection is borrowed from IMKL, Appliance and Meter are defined by SAREF.Note that Appliance, but also Control System and Sensor Systems are. The latter are non-disjoint with Toestel, a Sensor bvb so additionally when Toestel are typed and combine characteristics of both classes. Appliances as well as Connections are also potentially Platforms, ttz that they can host other Systems, e.g. an Meter on a Connection, or a Controller or Sensor on a Device.We also provide a reference to the class MachineLearningModel from ML-DCAT, so that a Sensor can be additionally typed as an instance of this class. For example, it can be indicated that the Observation is a prediction made by AI. Terms are reused from ISO19156-2023 OMS,SSN/SOSA-2025,SAREF,IMKLenMLD-DCAT.An energy management system (EMS) is an automated system that optimizes energy consumption in a building, home or industrial complex. This is done by real-time monitoring and measurement, analysis of trends and peak consumption and inefficiencies, optimization and saving by smart algorithms and AI, automatic control of devices and systems (heating, lighting).Earlier than describing the EMS itself, this application profile allows to document the actions carried out by it. These actions are Observations and Controls, e.g. the observation of the energy consumption of the appliance and its acquisition or disposal in function.These actions are described in a structured manner as provided for in ISO19156-2023 OMS, SSN/SOSA-2025 from which they were derived. This means that, in addition to the result vd Observation/Management, the Observation/ManageableKnowledge must be specified (e.g. consumption, on- or off-mode), as well as the Object that has that feature (e.g. Connection or Device), the procedure followed during observation or control (e.g. on the basis of which energy consumption threshold a Device is set) and finally the Control System or the Sensor used in it.The ability to indicate the Input used in the Observation/Management procedure allows a sequence of Observation and/or Control actions to be described, where the result of an Observation/Management constitutes the input for a subsequent Observation/Management procedure. We also provide Observation collection from SSN/SOA so that Observations can be grouped together as Input. Input can also be a threshold or rule (the modelling of rules is outside scope, see RIF,N3,RuleML,IDPedm).This approach allows a lot of EMS use-cases to be described. For example, the shutdown of a production line (Control) based on the day-ahead electricity price (Observation) and a threshold (Input vd Control) and the calculation of the savings (Observation) based on the average consumption of the production line (Observation). Or classifying a building (Observation) according to its average heat loss (Observation), based on the measured heat loss of several representative spaces in the building (multiple Observations). See the data examples in github (explained in the modelling report) that illustrate this principle.The observedObject/controlledObject may be an instance of Object or an instance of any class by substitution of Object. However, if applicable, a subclass thereof may be used, ttz an instance of Connection or Device (or Subclass Meter of Device). Connection is borrowed from IMKL, Appliance and Meter are defined by SAREF.Note that Appliance, but also Control System and Sensor Systems are. The latter are non-disjoint with Toestel, a Sensor bvb so additionally when Toestel are typed and combine characteristics of both classes. Appliances as well as Connections are also potentially Platforms, ttz that they can host other Systems, e.g. an Meter on a Connection, or a Controller or Sensor on a Device.We also provide a reference to the class MachineLearningModel from ML-DCAT, so that a Sensor can be additionally typed as an instance of this class. For example, it can be indicated that the Observation is a prediction made by AI. An energy management system (EMS) is an automated system that optimizes energy consumption in a building, home or industrial complex. This is done by real-time monitoring and measurement, analysis of trends and peak consumption and inefficiencies, optimization and saving by smart algorithms and AI, automatic control of devices and systems (heating, lighting).Earlier than describing the EMS itself, this application profile allows to document the actions carried out by it. These actions are Observations and Controls, e.g. the observation of the energy consumption of the appliance and its acquisition or disposal in function.These actions are described in a structured manner as provided for in ISO19156-2023 OMS, SSN/SOSA-2025 from which they were derived. This means that, in addition to the result vd Observation/Management, the Observation/ManageableKnowledge must be specified (e.g. consumption, on- or off-mode), as well as the Object that has that feature (e.g. Connection or Device), the procedure followed during observation or control (e.g. on the basis of which energy consumption threshold a Device is set) and finally the Control System or the Sensor used in it.The ability to indicate the Input used in the Observation/Management procedure allows a sequence of Observation and/or Control actions to be described, where the result of an Observation/Management constitutes the input for a subsequent Observation/Management procedure. We also provide Observation collection from SSN/SOA so that Observations can be grouped together as Input. Input can also be a threshold or rule (the modelling of rules is outside scope, see RIF,N3,RuleML,IDPedm).This approach allows a lot of EMS use-cases to be described. For example, the shutdown of a production line (Control) based on the day-ahead electricity price (Observation) and a threshold (Input vd Control) and the calculation of the savings (Observation) based on the average consumption of the production line (Observation). Or classifying a building (Observation) according to its average heat loss (Observation), based on the measured heat loss of several representative spaces in the building (multiple Observations). See the data examples in github (explained in the modelling report) that illustrate this principle.The observedObject/controlledObject may be an instance of Object or an instance of any class by substitution of Object. However, if applicable, a subclass thereof may be used, ttz an instance of Connection or Device (or Subclass Meter of Device). Connection is borrowed from IMKL, Appliance and Meter are defined by SAREF.Note that Appliance, but also Control System and Sensor Systems are. The latter are non-disjoint with Toestel, a Sensor bvb so additionally when Toestel are typed and combine characteristics of both classes. Appliances as well as Connections are also potentially Platforms, ttz that they can host other Systems, e.g. an Meter on a Connection, or a Controller or Sensor on a Device.We also provide a reference to the class MachineLearningModel from ML-DCAT, so that a Sensor can be additionally typed as an instance of this class. For example, it can be indicated that the Observation is a prediction made by AI. An energy management system (EMS) is an automated system that optimizes energy consumption in a building, home or industrial complex. This is done by real-time monitoring and measurement, analysis of trends and peak consumption and inefficiencies, optimization and saving by smart algorithms and AI, automatic control of devices and systems (heating, lighting).Earlier than describing the EMS itself, this application profile allows to document the actions carried out by it. These actions are Observations and Controls, e.g. the observation of the energy consumption of the appliance and its acquisition or disposal in function.These actions are described in a structured manner as provided for in IS